Scheduling tasks fed from Python data
=====================================

This example schedules five kitchen tasks.  Each task has a duration and
needs one resource.  Some tasks must follow others.  Python holds the data
and asks Curry for a schedule that fits a horizon of a given length.  It
grows the horizon until a schedule exists and draws that schedule as a
Gantt chart.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

Constraint solving by search.  The schedule in

    starts = [ (n, anyOf [0 .. horizon - d]) | (n, d, _) <- tasks ]

gives each task anyOf the start slots that keep it inside the horizon.  The
call anyOf xs is a choice among the elements of xs.  So the one expression
starts means every assignment at once.

Lazy generate and test.  The guard

    all ordered prec && all noOverlap pairs

keeps the assignments where every precedence holds and no two tasks on one
resource overlap.  A failed guard is not an error.  It produces no value.
The Curry side only states the constraints.  It has no loop, no search
state, and no backtracking code.  The runtime does the search.

The order of the guards matters.  The runtime makes a choice only when the
guard needs its value.  The precedence check comes first.  It forces the
starts along the chain of tasks, and a task that starts too soon fails
before any later task gets a start.  The resource check comes second and
sees only the assignments that survived.

Optimization by failure.  A horizon that is too short has no schedule, so
the call yields zero values.  Python counts upward from the longest task.
The first horizon with a schedule is the shortest one.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The tasks and the precedences are Python data: a list of (name, duration,
resource) tuples and a list of (before, after) pairs.  The driver go.py
passes them to the Curry function schedule with the horizon.  curry.eval
converts them with curry.expr: tuples become Curry tuples, lists become
Curry lists, and ints become Curry Ints.

A Python str of length one converts to a Char.  So the driver passes every
name as a list of characters, through the helper text().

One call,

    curry.eval(Sched.schedule, tasks, precedences, horizon, converter='topython')

is a generator of schedules.  The driver takes the first one with next() and
discards the generator.  This stops the search.  A horizon with no schedule
yields zero values, so next() returns the default None, and the driver tries
the next horizon.  The converter turns the schedule into a Python list of
(str, int) pairs.  The driver sorts it by start slot and draws the chart.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run

To try other tasks, edit TASKS and PRECEDENCES at the top of go.py.  If
several schedules of the same length exist, the first one found may differ
between the backends.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  The driver prints
the tasks and the precedences first.  Then it prints one line per horizon:

    horizon 8: none
    horizon 9: mix@0 bake@2 cool@5 frost@7 pack@8

The chain mix, bake, cool, frost, pack has a total duration of 9.  So no
schedule fits 8 slots, and exactly one fits 9.  The chart follows, with one
row per task in order of start:

    slot   012345678
    mix    ##.......  bowl
    bake   ..###....  oven
    cool   .....##..  rack
    frost  .......#.  bowl
    pack   ........#  table

The output is identical on both backends.


5. Limits
---------

The work depends on how early the guards prune.  The precedences prune
early, so a chain of tasks is cheap.  A chain of eight tasks on four
resources took 0.7 s on the Python backend and 0.04 s on the C++ backend.
Without precedences only the resource check prunes.  It forces the start of
every task that shares a resource, so the work grows with the slot count to
the power of the task count.  Five tasks on one resource and no precedences
took 11 s on the Python backend and 0.4 s on the C++ backend.  Six such
tasks did not finish in 45 s on the Python backend and ran out of memory
under a 2 GB cap on the C++ backend.

A warm run of ./run took 0.5 s on the Python backend and 0.2 s on the C++
backend.  The first run also compiles the module.  It took about 10 s on
each backend.
